`hooks/enforce-agent-contract.sh` could not be satisfied and did not bound. Its transcript scan read `message.content` only as an ARRAY, but a text-only assistant turn — which every compliant final report is — serializes it as a STRING, so the report was never read and an earlier mid-run turn was judged instead: an agent was told it violated the contract forever, no matter how many times it complied. It also read `transcript_path` rather than the `agent_transcript_path` the measured payload table lists for `SubagentStop` and the repo's other consumer of that event uses — an ambiguity removed, not a second confirmed misread, since the consumer's own array-shape experiment could only have worked against the agent's own transcript. Its loop-guard marker was deleted on both exit paths, turning "exactly one forced continuation" into an unbounded block/pass alternation — measured 2/0/2 over three stops, now 2/0/0. Consumer cost, correctly re-attributed from agent misjudgement to this hook: one run notified the lead four times, and an agent ran a ten-minute gate including a plugin deploy into a live game install on a task that changed zero source files, explicitly to satisfy the stop hook. The posture is v2.2.1's, mirrored onto reporting: a contract that cannot determine whether the agent is finished must not demand the artifact of finishing. Terminal-stop detection was investigated and is NOT available — `SubagentStop` carries no `stop_hook_active` and no resume flag, and a trailing `tool_use` block is not a substitute discriminator — so no heuristic was invented; the sticky marker is what bounds the cost of not knowing. Every transcript reader in the repo was audited for the same string-vs-array assumption (the mirror of it produced a wrong figure in the v2.1 mining pass): only this hook was wrong; `retro-ledger.sh` matches `tool_result` blocks, which exist only in array content, and is correct as written. The hook had no behavioural fixture at all — that is the gap all three defects lived in — so `scripts/test-hooks.sh` gains 12, including the three-stop loop-guard sequence over a shared marker directory. Downstream: re-copy `hooks/enforce-agent-contract.sh`, one file, no mirror by design. 12 hook scripts + `hooks/lib/`, 8 skills; 261 consistency assertions, 321 hook fixtures.
